From bd5a9830b1443c41bcf1b26ef7e48df6c6614764 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Boyd <danieljboyd@icloud.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 11:36:51 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] GSTheme: let a theme turn GNUstep-drawn window decorations on

A theme that draws the window decorations itself, as one drawing GNOME's
header bar does, needs GSBackHandlesWindowDecorations NO: the backend
then leaves the window manager's decorations off and the gui draws its
own.  A theme can set that default in the GSThemeDomain of its
Info-gnustep.plist, but the backend reads it when the display server is
created in -[NSApplication _init], before the theme is loaded, so the
theme's value arrives too late and every user had to set the default by
hand.

GSThemeInstallBackendDefaults(), called just before the display server
is created, reads the GSThemeDomain of the theme the GSTheme default
names, without loading the theme's code, and installs its
GSBackHandlesWindowDecorations (and only that) as the GSThemeDomain
volatile domain, placed in the search list where -[GSTheme activate]
puts it, above the registration domain.  When the theme is activated
later its whole domain replaces this one.  Nothing is installed when the
user has set GSBackHandlesWindowDecorations or the older
GSX11HandlesWindowDecorations in any domain other than the registration
domain (a value an application or library merely registered is not the
user's choice), when a theme has already put its domain in place, for
the GNUstep theme, or when the theme can't be found or doesn't set the
default.  The theme bundle lookup moves out of +loadThemeNamed: into
GSThemePathForFileName() so both use the same one.

Testing: Tests/gui/GSTheme/backendDefaults.m makes two theme bundles in
a temporary directory, one with the default in its GSThemeDomain and one
without, names them by absolute path in GSTheme, and calls the function
directly, so it needs no display.  It checks each case that must
install nothing (no GSTheme, the GNUstep theme, the user's value for
either default, a theme without the default, a theme that isn't there),
then that the theme's value is installed alone in GSThemeDomain, reads
back as NO although YES is registered for it, sits in the search list
once and above the registration domain, and stays that way when the
function is called again; and that +loadThemeNamed: still finds a theme
by path after the lookup moved.  Defaults are set in the argument
domain, for the test process only.  Without this change the test
doesn't run (the function doesn't exist); with it all 11 checks pass.
The registered-default check found a bug in an earlier draft, which
tested -objectForKey: and so let any registered value block the theme.

AI assistance: this change, its test and this description were written
with Claude (Anthropic's Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
